Jump directly to the interactions by [clicking here](#mount-frame).

It's the fall of 2026 - my second last semester at Harvard. As part of the Design Engineering program, I'm taking a class called Parts of Speech: Reimagining Computing Through Verbs at the GSD, taught by [Jenny Rodenhouse](). I'll attach a brief excerpt from the syllabus here:

>Every interface is built from actions. We boot, click, swipe, friend, and cancel; each verb carries a history and a worldview about who and what computing is for. With large language models (LLMs) and text-to-form tools, a word can call a world into being in seconds, so choosing how to order a few letters is also a way of building one. This seminar takes verbs as its subject, studying how form and time direct a person to act.

Each week, we do a *verb study*. This involves:

1. Selecting a verb in response to a particular era of computing, researching its origins, etymology, and history through four lenses—illustrative, abstract, emotive and antithetical—and representing these through a type study.
2. Deconstructing each artifact through elements of design: line, color, shape, form, space and composition, texture.
3. Sketching the interaction at a low fidelity, before building an interactive version of it using code.

I thought it'd be nice to [document](https://notes.andymatuschak.org/zCMhncA1iSE74MKKYQS5PBZ) the prototypes I make here, along with a bit of my thinking process.

**Week 1:**

We had to select a verb associate with the "command" era of computing (ie. pre-GUI), specifically in response to the verb "boot".

I chose the verb "mount". In computing terms, the definition is as follows:

*"To make a disk or disk drive available for use on a computer."*

I was interested in "mount" because of the multitude of other meanings the verb has:

- "To gradually increase, rise, or get bigger; to go up or onto."
- "To attach something to a wall, in a frame, etc., so that it can be looked at or used."
- "To get on a horse, bicycle, etc. in order to ride."

Here are the type studies I did based on the 4 definitions:

<PhotoGallery items={typeStudies} label="Mount type studies" aspectRatio="3 / 2" fit="cover" />

I was most interested in exploring an interaction based on "mount" that involves attaching something to a wall or in a frame, since to me it felt like the definition that is closest to being the spiritual root of the [computing](https://columbia.edu/cu/computinghistory/media.html) related definition.

All that being said, here is the first interaction I designed:

<Prototype id="mount-frame" title="Mount: picture frame" document={frame} compact />

This first interaction was fun, but rather plain, so I wanted to see if I could extend it a bit further, which led to this:

<Prototype id="mount-gallery" title="Mount: gallery" document={gallery} />

The images are sourced from a few different art archives and each refresh provides a different set of them, loosely organized into eras. I kind of got carried away making this (something I'm thinking about for IDEP is how the sunk cost fallacy still applies to vibe-coded prototypes: even when you haven't even sunk that much work into something, throwing it away is hard!)

While it is a cool interaction, it's probably closer to the verb "align" than "mount". That being said, it was a fun start to the class, and hopefully I can get better at this over the semester.
